<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan de Witt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1625–1672</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan de Witt was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> of Holland from 1653 to 1672 and the leading statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-stadtholderless-period">First Stadtholderless Period</a>. A skilled mathematician and financier, he restored the Republic's finances, built up its navy and kept the house of Orange from power. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> attacked in 1672 he was blamed for the disaster and was lynched with his brother Cornelis by a mob in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt.jpg" alt="Johan de Witt" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adriaen Hanneman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanneman,_Adriaen_-_Johan_de_Witt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
